    "evidence_text": "This article reviews homocysteine metabolism, causes of hyperhomocysteinemia, the pathophysiological findings of this disorder, and epidemiological studies of homocysteine and vascular disease.\n\nScreening for hyperhomocysteinemia should be considered for patients at high risk for vascular disease or abnormalities of homocysteine metabolism.\n\nFor primary prevention of vascular disease, treatment of patients with homocysteine levels of 14 micromol/L or higher should be considered.\n\nFor secondary prevention, treatment of patients with homocysteine levels of 11 micromol/L or higher should be considered.\n\nRESULTS Levels of homocyst(e)ine were higher in cases than in controls (11.1 +/- 4.0 [SD] vs 10.5 +/-\n\nBecause high levels can often be easily treated with vitamin supplements, homocyst(e)ine may be an independent, modifiable risk factor.\n\nCONTEXT Hyperhomocysteinemia is caused by genetic and lifestyle influences, including low intakes of folate and vitamin B6.\n\nBACKGROUND Increased plasma homocysteine is associated with coronary artery disease, peripheral vascular disease and venous thrombosis.",
